  "image_name": "5bf43fd64145f7657e4a0d61ccd27565.png",
  "prompt_text": "The figure summarizes major machine-learning strategies for molecular design and then shows an example workflow and outputs. Across the top and middle, four common generative frameworks are sketched. In reinforcement learning, a generative network proposes molecules from a latent space and a predictive model returns a reward that guides improvement. In diffusion models, molecules are mapped through a latent representation with a forward diffusion process and then regenerated through iterative denoising. In a generative adversarial network, a generator produces candidate molecules from latent variables while a discriminator compares generated and real molecules and provides a loss signal. In a variational autoencoder, molecules are encoded into a compact latent space and decoded back into reconstructed molecules.\n\nThe lower-left panel combines these ideas into a practical discovery loop: training data and known actives are used to train generative models, molecules are sampled, scored with a QSAR model, and reinforcement appears to feed high-scoring results back to improve generation; enrichment, few-shot learning, and transfer learning are also indicated. The lower-right panel shows two example chemical structures, illustrating the kind of candidate compounds such methods can produce. Overall, the figure highlights latent-space-based generation and iterative optimization of drug-like molecules.",
